<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
    <artifactId>eyeandsickle-parent</artifactId>
    <version>${revision}</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>eyeandsickle-protocol</artifactId>
  <name>The Eye and Sickle :: Protocol</name>
  <description>
    Wire types shared by client and server, plus the item-provenance verifier.

    Named `protocol` rather than `common` on purpose: `common` is a junk drawer
    that names no rule, so a heat threshold or a price constant can drift in
    unnoticed. `protocol` names one — "this is what crosses the wire" — which
    makes a game rule sitting here read as obviously wrong.

    THE CHARTER (enforced by ArchitectureRulesTest, not just by good intentions):
      * Records, enums and sealed interfaces describing what crosses the wire.
      * The provenance chain verifier, which docs/architecture/04 §6.2 requires
        to run client-side and offline, byte-identically to the server's copy.
      * NO game rules. No thresholds, no prices, no yields, no recovery curves,
        no gate evaluation. Those are authoritative state and belong to the
        server (Invariant I14 — the client is never authoritative over anything
        a cheater would forge).
      * No Spring, no JavaFX, no persistence, no clock, no randomness.
  </description>

  <dependencies>
    <!--
      RFC 8785 JSON Canonicalization Scheme, the reference JVM implementation.
      docs/architecture/04 §4 fixes JCS as the canonicalization. Zero compile
      dependencies of its own (verified), which is what keeps this module's
      resolved tree austere enough to jlink into the client later.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.erdtman</groupId>
      <artifactId>java-json-canonicalization</artifactId>
      <version>${json-canonicalization.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Jackson 3 (`tools.jackson.*`, NOT `com.fasterxml.jackson.*`). Pinned to
      the same version Spring Boot 4.1 manages, so one Jackson serves the whole
      reactor. Jackson 2 and 3 have different package names and CAN coexist —
      which is exactly how a project ends up shipping both by accident.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>tools.jackson.core</groupId>
      <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
      <version>${jackson.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      BouncyCastle. REVERSES this module's long-standing "no crypto dependency"
      note, on explicit direction 2026-08-02, and the old reasoning is recorded
      here because it was sound when written and is no longer true.

      It said: "BouncyCastle would be a 5 MB dependency for an algorithm the
      platform already has, and it would have to be jlinked into the client
      image for nothing." Two facts overtook it.

        1. secp256k1. Most did:plc accounts sign with it and SunEC CANNOT verify
           it (measured on OpenJDK 26: the curve name resolves, a key builds,
           initVerify SUCCEEDS, and only verify() refuses). See
           docs/architecture/10-oauth-and-did-resolution.md section 5.1.
        2. HPKE (RFC 9180), which backs the secure channel. SunJCE has it only
           on JDK 26+; this project targets 25.

      So it is no longer "for an algorithm the platform already has" - it is for
      two the platform does not have on the target runtime.

      MEASURED COST: 8.1 MB, not the 5 MB the old note estimated. It lands in
      the five client uber jars and the jpackage image (~135 MB, so roughly 6%).

      Chosen over noise-java and Tink because it is an IETF standard (RFC 9180)
      implementation from a long-lived, widely-reviewed library, and it was
      already on the server classpath for reason 1.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.bouncycastle</groupId>
      <artifactId>bcprov-jdk18on</artifactId>
      <version>${bouncycastle.version}</version>
    </dependency>


    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>protocol-stays-austere</id>
            <goals><goal>enforce</goal></goals>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <bannedDependencies>
                  <message>
                    eyeandsickle-protocol must not depend on Spring, JavaFX, a
                    persistence provider, or a logging backend. It is shared by
                    a JavaFX desktop client and a Spring Boot server, and it is
                    a candidate for a jlink runtime image. If you need one of
                    these, the code belongs in server or client, not here.
                  </message>
                  <searchTransitive>true</searchTransitive>
                  <excludes>
                    <exclude>org.springframework:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.springframework.boot:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.openjfx:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>jakarta.persistence:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.hibernate*:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>ch.qos.logback:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.apache.logging.log4j:*</exclude>
                  </excludes>
                </bannedDependencies>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
